# /etc/vector/conf.d — manual Vector drop-ins
#
# Any *.yaml here is merged ON TOP of /etc/vector/vector.yaml by the vector
# service (vector multi-config: later files win). This is the supported way
# to add sinks (e.g. a remote log endpoint) WITHOUT touching the package-
# managed base config.
#
# Rules:
#   - component IDs must not collide with the base config (sources: syslog,
#     internal_logs, dinit_services, incus_logs, user_session_logs; sinks:
#     messages, auth_log, daemon_log, kernel_log, sudo_log, firewalld_log,
#     user_session_log)
#   - reference the sources above as inputs; never re-declare them
#   - validate before restarting: vector validate --skip-healthchecks
# The session-runtime source (user_session_logs) tails /run/user/*/*.log —
# a session tool writing $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/<name>.log is picked up
# automatically; ship it with a drop-in sink when needed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REMOTE SHIPPING — HOW TO CONFIGURE IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Nothing is shipped remotely out of the box. These are the instructions for
# adding a remote destination yourself; the base config stays untouched.
#
# 1. Who merges the drop-ins
#    - dinit: the wrapper /usr/lib/dinit/vector merges every
#      /etc/vector/conf.d/*.yaml after the base config. Nothing to enable.
#    - OpenRC: the Alpine initd does NOT merge them. Add the file explicitly
#      to /etc/conf.d/vector:
#          command_args="--config /etc/vector/conf.d/10-remote.yaml"
#      (one --config per file) and restart the service.
#
# 2. File placement and permissions
#    Drop files as root, mode 0644, name <NN>-<purpose>.yaml (the number just
#    keeps the merge order readable). The directory is package-managed and
#    root-owned — do not chown it.
#
# 3. What a drop-in contains
#    Only NEW components that reference the existing sources. Typical shape:
#
#      sinks:
#        remote_syslog:
#          type: socket
#          inputs: [syslog, dinit_services, user_session_logs]
#          address: "logs.example.net:6514"
#          mode: tcp
#          tls:
#            enabled: true
#            verify_certificate: true
#          encoding:
#            codec: json
#
#      sinks:
#        remote_http:
#          type: http
#          inputs: [syslog, dinit_services]
#          uri: "https://ingest.example.net/feralos"
#          method: post
#          auth:
#            strategy: bearer
#            token: "${FERALOS_INGEST_TOKEN}"
#          encoding:
#            codec: json
#
#    `inputs` may name any base source. Avoid shipping `syslog` wholesale if
#    you do not want auth/sudo content at the remote end — call out only the
#    sources you need, or insert a `transforms` block between source and sink
#    and reference that transform in `inputs` instead.
#
# 4. Secrets
#    Vector interpolates ${VAR} in the RAW config text — including comments.
#    An undefined variable makes the process exit 78 at startup, so:
#      - never write ${...} inside a comment (it is still interpolated);
#      - keep the token OUT of the YAML (files here are world-readable) and
#        provide the variable through the service environment:
#          dinit:  /etc/dinit.d/config/vector.conf (KEY=VALUE lines; the
#                  package ships it 0644 — chmod 0600 once it holds a secret;
#                  dinit reads it as root and passes the variables down)
#          OpenRC: /etc/conf.d/vector (sourced by the initd; chmod 0600 too)
#    If you do not want the variable in a file at all, use a file-based
#    credential instead (e.g. a sink option that reads a path) and protect
#    that path with permissions.
#
# 5. Durable delivery
#    Memory buffers are lost on restart. To buffer on disk, add to the sink:
#          buffer:
#            type: disk
#            max_size: 1073741824
#    The buffer lives under data_dir (/var/lib/vector), owned by the vector
#    user — no extra setup.
#
# 6. Validate, restart, verify
#      vector validate --skip-healthchecks \
#          /etc/vector/vector.yaml /etc/vector/conf.d/10-remote.yaml
#      dinitctl restart vector        # or: rc-service vector restart
#      vector top                     # components + throughput
#    A syntax error keeps the previous process running (the restart is
#    rejected); a missing env var stops it with exit 78.
#
# 7. Removing a remote destination
#    Delete the file from /etc/vector/conf.d/ and restart the service. Keep a
#    copy outside the directory if you want to re-enable it later — a *.yaml
#    left behind stays active.
